---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const modCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Three statements, one engine', note: 'Model the income statement (sales → profit), balance sheet (what funds it), cash flow (what actually arrives) — linked by formulas. Every assumption visible in one cell.' },
    { math: '2 · Drivers, not patches', note: 'Model the BUSINESS logic: units × price, growth rates, margins as functions of scale. Hard-coding outcomes makes the model a slideshow; driver-based models answer questions.' },
    { math: '3 · Scenarios: the point of the exercise', note: 'Base / optimistic / pessimistic cases, switched at one cell. The output is a RANGE of outcomes and which assumptions matter most — sensitivity analysis is the model\'s real product.' },
    { math: '4 · Hygiene is survival', note: 'Separate inputs from calculations; one formula per row; colour-code inputs; check the balance sheet balances. Spreadsheet errors are common and expensive (the EU\'s austerity spreadsheet error is the famous case).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/advanced/financial-modelling-for-managers">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate lessons gave you the raw components: what the income statement, balance sheet and cash-flow
    forecast each show, and what the ratios mean. A financial model is what happens when those documents stop
    being snapshots and become <strong>one linked machine</strong>: you change an assumption — price, headcount,
    collection days — and the three statements, the ratios and the funding need all update coherently. Its
    purpose is decision support: testing "what if we cut price 10%?" before the real money moves. This lesson
    builds a small model end to end, then uses it the way managers actually should — as a laboratory for
    scenarios and a diagnostic for the business.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="statements, drivers, scenarios, hygiene" widget="equationStep" config={modCfg} />

  <h2>The income statement block: drivers, not numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Every block starts with <strong>drivers</strong> — the assumptions a manager can actually control or
    forecast — and every figure below them is a formula. The classic driver pair is units and price. Suppose we
    model a firm selling 100,000 units at 20 each: revenue = 2,000,000. Cost of goods sold is modelled per
    unit (say 12), giving COGS of 1,200,000 and gross profit of 800,000 — a 40% gross margin. Operating
    expenses are semi-fixed: 500,000, largely salaries and rent that do not scale with the 100,001st unit.
    EBIT is 300,000, a 15% operating margin. Below the line: interest of 32,000 on 400,000 of debt at 8%
    (drivers: the debt balance and the rate — never a typed number), pre-tax profit 268,000, tax at 25% =
    67,000, <strong>net income 201,000</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the whole skeleton, and its virtue is that every number on the page is now <em>explained</em>.
    When a manager asks "can we afford a sales push?", the model answers precisely: 10,000 more units at a
    8-per-unit contribution margin (20 − 12) add 80,000 of gross profit against whatever the incremental
    marketing costs — a question a pile of past income statements cannot answer.
  </p>

  <h2>The balance sheet block: where cash becomes the plug</h2>
  <p>
    The statement of financial position is modelled as the <em>consequence</em> of operations, not a separate
    list. Working-capital items are driven off the income statement by days: with sales of 2,000,000 and 37
    days' sales outstanding, receivables ≈ 2,000,000 × 37 / 365 ≈ 200,000; with COGS of 1,200,000 and 46 days
    of inventory, inventory ≈ 150,000; with 37 days of payables, payables ≈ 120,000. Add cash of 100,000 and
    fixed assets of 650,000: total assets = 1,100,000. Liabilities: payables 120,000 + debt 400,000 + other
    50,000 = 570,000. Equity must then be 530,000 for the sheet to balance — and equity is not typed in; it is
    opening equity plus net income minus dividends, rolled forward year by year.
  </p>
  <p>
    The professional technique is to forecast every asset and liability from drivers, choose the financing
    (debt, equity, dividends), and let <strong>cash be the plug</strong> — the one item that absorbs whatever
    the others imply. This turns the balance check into the model's lie detector: if the sheet will not balance,
    a formula is broken; if the plug goes <em>negative</em>, the model is telling you something real — the plan
    needs more funding. That is exactly the "needs and sources" logic of the financing decision: the model
    measures the gap; the manager chooses whether to fill it with retained earnings, an overdraft, a term loan
    or equity. The matching principle applies — short-term working-capital gaps want revolving credit; a
    machine that earns returns over ten years wants term debt or equity, not a bank overdraft that can be
    called in a bad month.
  </p>

  <h2>The cash-flow block: why profit is not cash</h2>
  <p>
    The cash statement exists because accrual accounting spreads events across periods that cash ignores. Our
    model firm is profitable and still cash-tight: it books the 2,000,000 of revenue while only ~1,800,000 of
    it has been collected (receivables grew), and it has spent 200,000 on equipment that never appears in the
    income statement this year. A four-week cash forecast makes the rhythm explicit. Week 1 opens with 150,000;
    receipts (mostly old invoices clearing) total 500,000 for the month, while payments run 400,000 to
    suppliers, 150,000 to staff, 50,000 to the landlord: closing cash 50,000 — solvent, but one late-paying
    customer away from an overdraft. Forecasts like this are built <em>weekly</em> in practice, because
    insolvency is a matter of dates, not totals; the corporate-finance lesson's cash conversion cycle is the
    summary statistic of the same physics (here, 37 + 46 − 37 = 46 days of cash locked in operations ≈
    2,000,000 × 46 / 365 ≈ 250,000).
  </p>

  <h2>Analysis of accounts: ratios as the model's dashboard</h2>
  <p>
    Ratios stop being memorised formulas once you can watch them move. From the model above: gross margin
    40%; operating margin 15%; net margin 201 / 2,000 ≈ <strong>10.1%</strong>. Return on capital employed =
    EBIT / (equity + debt) = 300,000 / 930,000 ≈ <strong>32.3%</strong> — comfortably above any plausible WACC,
    so the business is creating value. Gearing = 400 / 530 ≈ <strong>75%</strong>; interest cover = 300 / 32 ≈
    <strong>9.4×</strong>. The DuPont decomposition then explains <em>where</em> the 37.9% return on equity comes
    from: net margin (10.1%) × asset turnover (2,000 / 1,100 = 1.82) × equity multiplier (1,100 / 530 = 2.08) ≈
    37.9%. Reading it this way tells you which lever moved: a firm can match a rival's ROE with thin margins and
    fast turnover (a grocer) or fat margins and slow turnover (a jeweller) — and the two businesses respond to
    completely different managerial fixes. Variance analysis closes the loop: when actual results arrive, every
    gap between forecast and actual identifies <em>which driver was wrong</em>, and the next model iteration is
    informed, not improvised.
  </p>

  <h2>Scenario analysis: the model's real product</h2>
  <p>
    A single forecast is a confession of false confidence. The working model is run as
    <strong>base / upside / downside</strong> cases switched from one scenario cell, and then, more surgically,
    <strong>sensitivity analysis</strong> — flexing one driver at a time to see which assumptions the plan
    actually lives and dies on. Here is what our model reveals. Cut price 10% (to 18) with volume unchanged:
    revenue falls to 1,800,000, COGS stays 1,200,000, so gross profit drops 200,000 and <strong>EBIT falls from
    300,000 to 100,000 — a 66.7% profit hit from a 10% price move</strong>. Raise volume 10% instead (110,000
    units at 20): gross profit rises 80,000 and EBIT rises to 380,000 — +26.7%. Price dominates volume in this
    business because COGS is variable and opex is fixed: the elasticity arithmetic of a discount is brutal.
    A downside case stacking price −10% with volume −10% produces an EBIT of 0 (1.8M revenue, 1.2M COGS less
    0.6M opex... exactly breakeven before interest), which is the number the bank will ask about. The output of
    good modelling is this ranked list of vulnerabilities — management's attention should follow it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the model's job is to predict the future." Its job is to make the
  <em>consequences of assumptions</em> inescapable. Anyone can produce a single impressive forecast; the analyst's
  contribution is showing which three assumptions carry the whole plan, and how wrong each can be before the
  financing breaks.</p>

  <h2>Variance analysis: the conversation with reality, worked</h2>
  <p>
    Budget-versus-actual review is where the model meets the world, and the professional technique is
    decomposition — splitting a total variance into its drivers so each gets an owner. Worked example: the
    model forecast 100,000 units at 20 (revenue 2,000,000); actual came in at 105,000 units at 19
    (revenue 1,995,000). The headline variance is a trivial −5,000 — and the headline is a lie. Split it:
    the <strong>volume effect</strong> at budget price is +5,000 units × 20 = <strong>+100,000</strong>;
    the <strong>price effect</strong> on actual volume is −1 × 105,000 = <strong>−105,000</strong>; sum:
    −5,000. Now the story is visible: the sales team over-delivered by 5% and someone gave away roughly
    the entire gain on price. The questions become pointed — was the discount a deliberate volume play
    (and did it hit its volume target elsewhere?), or margin leakage? The same decomposition runs down the
    cost side (usage variance: more material per unit than the standard; price variance: paying more per
    unit than standard), and each branch carries the analysis to a decision rather than a shrug. This loop
    — forecast, actual, decompose, revise the drivers — is the model's real operating rhythm; a model
    never re-forecast is a model nobody trusts.
  </p>

  <h2>The three uses, distinguished</h2>
  <p>
    The same three-statement machinery serves three distinct audiences, and confusing them wastes months.
    A <strong>forecast</strong> projects the future under stated assumptions (the scenarios above); a
    <strong>budget</strong> commits the organisation to targets for the coming year — which means it embeds
    negotiation, not just estimation (the sales director's number is a promise, the CFO's a test); and a
    <strong>valuation</strong> estimates what the whole business is worth — free cash flows discounted at the
    WACC from the corporate-finance lesson, with the model's drivers becoming the valuation's assumptions and
    the scenario range becoming the honest valuation range. The craft differences are real: forecasts want
    driver realism, budgets want accountability and monthly checkpoints, valuations want terminal assumptions
    defended explicitly (what growth can continue forever? — almost never the growth you have). A manager who
    can look at any spreadsheet and say "this is a forecast dressed as a budget" — assumptions without owners,
    or targets without mechanisms — has already extracted most of what financial modelling has to teach.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Hygiene is not housekeeping; it is risk management.</strong> The canonical horror stories are
    empirical: Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 claim that growth collapses past 90% public-debt-to-GDP collapsed
    itself in 2013 when Herndon, Ash and Pollin found the Excel averages had silently skipped rows in the
    sample; and the European Commission's 2015 impact assessment on austerity, where rows left out of a sum
    overstated the growth payoff of fiscal consolidation. Neither was a conceptual error — both were
    spreadsheet hygiene failures that shaped policy debates. The professional defences are unglamorous and
    non-negotiable: inputs in a separate, colour-coded block; one formula per row or column; no hard-coded
    numbers inside formulas; a live balance check that must equal zero; and an independent person rebuilding
    one section from scratch.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three modelling techniques take you further. <strong>Circularity and iteration</strong>: interest expense
    depends on debt, debt depends on cash, cash depends on interest — real models enable iterative calculation
    or break the loop with a prior-period copy, and knowing which choice you made matters when the model
    oscillates. <strong>Monte Carlo</strong>: instead of three scenarios, draw each driver from a distribution
    thousands of times and report the distribution of outcomes — the honest answer to "how much can we
    borrow?" is a percentile, not a number. <strong>Model risk as a discipline</strong>: banks now employ
    validation teams whose entire job is attacking models the way testers attack software, a practice managers
    outside banking would do well to copy.
  </p>
  <p>
    The open frontier is behavioural: evidence keeps accumulating that forecasters anchor on last year's
    numbers and that teams treat the model's precision as authority. The deep skill this lesson leaves you
    with is therefore judgement about the machine — knowing that the model is a map drawn with the assumptions
    of its author, and building the scenario machinery that lets reality push back.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Rosenbaum &amp; Pearl, <em>Investment Banking</em>; Wall Street Prep / CFI modelling curricula; Herndon, Ash &amp; Pollin (2013); the European Commission austerity spreadsheet case (2015).</p></TopicPage>
